# Git Workflow — branch per change

`main` is protected, always deployable, and auto-deploys to Render on merge. **Never commit
feature work directly to `main`.**

## Branch per change

- Every change (one roadmap slice = one Linear issue) gets its own branch, cut from an
  up-to-date `main`. Name it `<type>/<issue-id>-<slug>` using a standard type prefix and the
  Linear issue identifier (so the PR auto-links to the issue) — no username prefix.
  - Prefixes: `feature/` (new slice/capability), `bugfix/` (defect on a non-released branch),
    `hotfix/` (urgent fix to released `main`), `chore/` (tooling/config/deps), `refactor/`,
    `docs/`.
  - Example: `feature/10x-6-account-authentication`.
- Do the whole change on that branch — including all phases.

## Within the branch

- Follow `/10x-implement`'s phase workflow as defined by that skill: implement phase-by-phase,
  commit per phase, and append the commit SHA to the plan's Progress rows. Do not invent a
  different per-phase branching or PR scheme.

## Finishing a change

- When the change is complete and works end-to-end, open one PR from the change branch → `main`
  (`gh pr create` with a HEREDOC Summary + Test plan). CI must be green before merge.
- Merging to `main` is the deploy event, so only a complete, user-working slice should land there.
- Never push or force-push directly to `main`.
